# Implement a Multi-Producer, Single-Consumer (MPSC) Queue in C++

For a MAANG Staff Engineer interview, a good implementation should demonstrate thread safety, condition variables, correct shutdown handling, and avoidance of busy waiting.

We'll implement a queue where:

- Multiple producers can concurrently push items into a shared queue.
- One consumer waits for items and processes them.
- `std::mutex` protects the queue and shared state.
- `std::condition_variable` wakes the consumer when work arrives or shutdown begins.
- A shutdown flag lets the consumer exit cleanly after all queued items are processed.

## 1. Complete C++ implementation

```

#include <chrono>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

class MPSCQueue {
private:
    std::queue<int> q;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    bool stopped = false;

public:
    // Called by multiple producer threads.
    bool push(int value) {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

            // Reject new work after shutdown begins.
            if (stopped) {
                return false;
            }

            q.push(value);
        }

        // Notify after releasing the mutex.
        cv.notify_one();
        return true;
    }

    // Called by the single consumer thread.
    bool pop(int& value) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        // Sleep until work arrives or shutdown begins.
        cv.wait(lock, [this] {
            return !q.empty() || stopped;
        });

        // Shutdown and no remaining work.
        if (q.empty()) {
            return false;
        }

        value = q.front();
        q.pop();
        return true;
    }

    // Prevent new work and wake the consumer.
    void shutdown() {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            stopped = true;
        }

        cv.notify_one();
    }
};

int main() {
    MPSCQueue queue;

    constexpr int NUM_PRODUCERS = 3;
    constexpr int ITEMS_PER_PRODUCER = 5;

    std::vector<std::thread> producers;

    for (int p = 0; p < NUM_PRODUCERS; ++p) {
        producers.emplace_back([p, &queue] {
            for (int i = 0; i < ITEMS_PER_PRODUCER; ++i) {
                int value = p * 100 + i;

                if (queue.push(value)) {
                    std::cout << "Producer " << p
                              << " pushed " << value << '\n';
                }
            }
        });
    }

    // Exactly one consumer.
    std::thread consumer([&queue] {
        int value;

        while (queue.pop(value)) {
            std::cout << "Consumer processed "
                      << value << '\n';
        }

        std::cout << "Consumer exiting\n";
    });

    // Ensure every producer has finished publishing work.
    for (auto& producer : producers) {
        producer.join();
    }

    // No more items will be produced.
    queue.shutdown();

    consumer.join();

    return 0;
}

```

Compile and run:

```
g++ -std=c++17 -pthread mpsc_queue.cpp -o mpsc_queue
./mpsc_queue
```

The output order may vary because producers run concurrently. Each successfully pushed item will be processed once, provided the program completes normally.

## 2. How the condition variable works

The most important part is the consumer's `pop()` method.

```
bool pop(int& value) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    cv.wait(lock, [this] {
        return !q.empty() || stopped;
    });

    if (q.empty()) {
        return false;
    }

    value = q.front();
    q.pop();
    return true;
}
```

Let's break it down.

### Step 1: Acquire the mutex

```
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
```

The consumer locks the mutex before checking the queue. This prevents concurrent producers from modifying the queue while it is being inspected.

### Step 2: Wait using a predicate

```
cv.wait(lock, [this] {
    return !q.empty() || stopped;
});
```

The predicate means:

- `!q.empty()` — an item is available.
- `stopped` — shutdown has started.

If neither condition is true, the consumer sleeps. While waiting, `cv.wait()` atomically releases the mutex and begins waiting. 
When awakened, it reacquires the mutex before checking the predicate again.

Why use a predicate instead of `cv.wait(lock)` alone?

Because condition variables can wake spuriously, and notifications can occur before a thread starts waiting. 
The predicate ensures that the consumer proceeds only when the shared state permits it.

### Step 3: Handle shutdown

```
if (q.empty()) {
    return false;
}
```

If shutdown has started and the queue is empty, the consumer exits.

If items remain in the queue, the consumer continues processing them even after shutdown begins.


## 4. Important concurrency considerations

| Concern                     | How the implementation handles it          |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Concurrent producers        | Mutex serializes queue modifications       |
| Consumer with no work       | Sleeps instead of busy-waiting             |
| Spurious wakeups            | Predicate-based `wait()`                   |
| Lost notifications          | Shared state is checked under the mutex    |
| Shutdown with pending items | Remaining items are drained                |
| Push after shutdown         | Rejected by `push()`                       |
| Deadlock risk               | RAII locks release the mutex automatically |

### Why `std::unique_lock` instead of `std::lock_guard`?

`condition_variable::wait()` requires a `std::unique_lock<std::mutex>` because it must temporarily release the mutex while the thread waits and reacquire it before returning.

A `std::lock_guard` cannot perform this unlock/relock operation.

### Why notify after unlocking?

```
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    q.push(value);
}
cv.notify_one();
```

This is a common pattern because it allows the consumer to acquire the mutex immediately after waking rather than contending with the producer that just inserted the item.

Notifying while holding the mutex can also be correct; unlocking first is a performance-oriented convention, not a correctness requirement.

## 5. Staff-level interview follow-up questions

Be prepared to discuss these extensions:

1. How would you implement bounded capacity? Add a second condition variable to block producers while the queue is full.
2. How would you support multiple consumers? Use `notify_one()` for newly available work and reconsider shutdown notifications so all waiting consumers can exit.
3. How would you implement timed waits? Use `cv.wait_for()` with a predicate.
4. Is this lock-free? No. The queue is thread-safe but mutex-based.
5. Can items be processed in producer order? The queue preserves insertion order, but concurrent producers have no guaranteed global ordering beyond the order in 
which their insertions acquire the mutex.
6. What happens if a producer throws while creating an item? RAII protects the mutex, but exception handling and item-delivery guarantees depend on the 
operation and application requirements.

Key interview takeaway: The hardest part is not inserting and removing elements. It is coordinating the queue's state, waiting correctly, and ensuring shutdown doesn't lose work or leave the consumer blocked indefinitely.
